WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.580
Network resources are finite.

2
00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:16.920
HTTP connections, sockets, DNS behavior, proxy settings, TLS sessions, remote service quotas, and connection pools all influence how an application behaves under normal load and during dependency problems.

3
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:22.540
A reliable C# service treats outbound communication as a managed resource.

4
00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.940
HttpClient lifetime deserves review because it is more than a request wrapper.

5
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:39.800
Handler lifetime, connection pooling, DNS refresh, proxy behavior, cookies, certificates, redirects, authentication state, and default headers can all be affected by how clients and handlers are created.

6
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:48.620
Good designs use long-lived clients with managed handler lifetime or IHttpClientFactory patterns that fit the application.

7
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:56.900
Creating and disposing clients for every request without review can contribute to socket pressure and unpredictable connection behavior.

8
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:07.560
At the same time, sharing a client too broadly can accidentally share headers, cookies, tokens, or authentication assumptions across unrelated outbound calls.

9
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:11.980
The right lifetime depends on the trust boundary and configuration needs of the dependency.

10
00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:17.060
Outbound calls should have timeouts, cancellation, and bounded concurrency.

11
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:24.760
A dependency that slows down should not cause an unlimited number of callers, queued operations, or background jobs to wait indefinitely.

12
00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:31.660
Timeouts should be paired with cleanup and safe error handling so the application remains consistent when a call does not complete.

13
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:36.980
Retries, fan-out, and polling can amplify load if they are not controlled.

14
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.480
Retry policies should use limits and spacing that respect the dependency.

15
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:53.820
Calls to external APIs, storage services, queues, identity systems, and internal services should consider capacity, rate limits, tenant boundaries, and operational agreements.

16
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.980
Network diagnostics should be safe by design.

17
00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:08.920
URLs with credentials, authorization headers, cookies, tokens, client certificates, sensitive request bodies, and raw response bodies should not be casually logged.

18
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:15.900
Logs should capture enough context to diagnose failures without spreading sensitive data into monitoring and support systems.
